  "slug": "myopia-genetic-a731",
  "summary": "Single-cell, organoid and multi-omic datasets are now resolving cell-context and regulatory mechanisms in neurological disease genes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is how disease-associated genes are regulated and contextualized across cell types, using organoids, single-cell sequencing and network analysis. Time-resolved regulomes from Alzheimer's brain organoids nominate 110 AD-specific master transcription factors, with KLF5 and KLF8 motifs and CREB3L2-ATF4 control. Proteogenomic work in the Alzheimer's fusiform gyrus catalogs non-canonical proteoforms, while tau is shown to bind neuronal mRNAs in an m6A- and hnRNPA2B1-linked manner, with RNA dysregulation in MAPT V337M iNeurons. Cellular context is emphasized as most neurodevelopmental disorder genes are not brain-preferential, and single-neuron sequencing shows roughly 26% of prenatal cortical neurons carry widespread CNVs largely eliminated perinatally. Other items extend this to epilepsy network convergence, mosaic organoid modeling, microphthalmia trios, Cd47 excitotoxicity and Darier disease fibroblast methylation.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-31"
}
